From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:27:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] What moves through the dust pushes it, and the dust fades
 with distance

Patch 0037 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user, 2026-10-08, on the dust of patches 0035 and 0036: "as Link runs through it, it needs to
... have realistic motion as the dust would be moving along with Link in that direction, like it
would be pushed, and then air would kind of flow out behind Link and ... bellow out"; "all things
that can receive a shadow or cast a shadow should also interact with the dust"; and "a distance
setting on how far out the stuff renders ... a gradual fade out rather than a harsh cutoff ... This
would control the end of the fade-off distance".

The program hands over, after the game's lights, a trail of samples of every actor that moved
(the game's own table of them, sampled a tenth of a second apart and kept for a second), each with
group bit 32: a position, the motion in world units a second, a reach and the age. Every light
loop passes them over. The air pass takes the few whose wake reaches the eye's ray and pushes
each mote it passes: carried along with a young sample's motion, then, as the sample ages and its
wake widens, spilled outward from the path and lifted a little, settling as the sample dies. A
mote is pushed no further than its own cell allows, so the march still finds it.

A new parameter group, tuneAmbience, carries where the ambience's fade with distance ends (the
Ambience distance row). The dust fades from a fifth of that distance to all of it, measured from
the eye: 0035 measured its fade to the surface behind a mote, so the motes in front of a near wall
faded out, and that is corrected here. A long distance takes longer steps, at most 128 to a ray.

